   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the tax law, in relation to the imposition of an occupancy tax\nin the city of Cortland; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon\nthe expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S5344",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5344-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §1202-ff, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5344\n\nSPONSOR: SEWARD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the tax law, in relation to the impo-\nsition of an occupancy tax in the city of Cortland; and providing for\nthe repeal of such provisions upon the expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo authorize the City of Cortland to enact, at local option, a room\noccupancy tax on transient room rentals.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the tax law by adding a new section, 1202-ff, (1)\nproviding authorization for the City of Cortland to impose a tax of up\nto three percent on hotel and motel room rentals; (2) designates\nofficial(s) for the collection and administration of said tax, person(s)\nliable for payment, recipients and method of collection;\n\n(3) provides an exemption from taxation to government entities, reli-\ngious, charitable and educational organizations and organizations for\nthe prevention of cruelty to children or animals; (4) provides for a\nprocess of review in accordance with civil practice law and rules; and\n(5) designates that revenues from such tax be paid to the treasury of\ncity of Cortland to be credited to and deposited in to a city hospital-\nity tax fund.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date with a sunset of September 1,\n2022.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPursuant to City of Cortland Common Council resolution  31 of 2019, this\nbill would grant the City of Cortland the home rule authority to enact a\n\"bed tax\" on hotel and motel room rentals. The receipts will be deposit-\ned in a \"hospitality tax fund\" for water, sewer and storm water infras-\ntructure repairs, the construction of pedestrian pathways and bicycle\nways to link motels to recreational and commercial facilities catering\nto travelers, improve streets' visual appeal, and support events and\nattractions that promote the city and region as a destination.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-18: A7852-B, advanced to 3rd reading calendar.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State of New York.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediate and shall expire and be deemed repealed on September 1, 2022.\nThe city will be required to enact its own local law.\n",
